course: dictionary
lesson: 6213
title: Words people use to talk to each other
level: primary-5
stars: 0
explain: Some words are used to speak to people, not just to describe things. Context tells you who is speaking and how they feel. «Now then, missy, stop that!» A grown-up is talking to a girl, and stop that tells you they are a bit cross.
explain: Missy is an informal word. It is a playful or teasing way to call a young girl: «Listen, missy!» The word listen and the exclamation mark show a firm, telling-off tone, not a friendly name like Lucy.
explain: Missy has an older use too. In old writing it meant a young lady, a miss. When you meet an unknown word, check the clues in the sentence and the feel of the text before you decide what it means.
fact: Missy is an informal noun: people use it when speaking, not in formal writing.
fact: «Missy» is a way to address a young girl, often in a playful, teasing or slightly cross way.
fact: «Little missy» and «young missy» are phrases you may hear, often said by grown-ups.
fact: In older use, missy meant a young lady, the same as miss.
fact: The tone matters: the same word can sound playful or a bit bossy, so look at the words and feelings around it.
exercise: In «Now then, missy, stop that!», what does missy mean? | A word for a grown man | *A playful or cross way of speaking to a girl | A cheerful song | A kind of bird
exercise: Which clue shows that «Listen, missy!» is not a friendly name? | The name is spelled with a y | Nothing in the sentence shows it | The word Listen with a capital letter only | *The word listen and the exclamation mark
exercise: Why is missy called an informal word? | It is only found in dictionaries | *It is used in everyday speaking, not formal writing | It is a formal title like Doctor | It is used in formal letters to the head teacher
test: In an old book, a writer says a girl is «a young missy». What did missy mean there? | Too tired to walk | A teacher who shouts | *A young lady, in older use | A pet cat
test: Which best describes the tone of «Listen, missy!»? | *Firm, a bit cross or playful | Friendly and calm | Very excited about a party | Sad and quiet
exam: Read: «Now then, missy, you're getting cheeky.» What does missy suggest about how the speaker feels? | The speaker is crying | The speaker is amazed by the girl's cooking | The speaker wants a sandwich | *The speaker is a bit cross or teasing the girl
